{"name":"Gilded Glamour","hex":"#956841","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gilded","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of gild"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the color or quality of gold.","Made of gold or covered by a thin layer of gold."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gilded"},{"term":"glamour","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Originally, enchantment; magic charm; especially, the effect of a spell that causes one to see objects in a form that differs from reality, typically to make filthy, ugly, or repulsive things seem beauteous.","Alluring beauty or charm (often with sex appeal)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To enchant; to bewitch.","↑ Postrel, Virginia (5 November 2013), “One: The Magic of Glamour”, in The Power of Glamour: Longing and the Art of Visual Persuasion, Simon & Schuster, →ISBN: “Reflecting this sense of the word, by 1902 Webster's included two new definitions: “a kind of haze in the air, causing things to appear different from what they really are”…”"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glamour"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gilding","blocks":[{"text":"Gilding is a decorative technique for applying a very thin coating of gold over solid surfaces such as metal, wood, porcelain, or stone. A gilded object is also described as \"gilt\". Where metal is gilded, the metal below was traditionally silver in the West, to make silver-gilt objects, but gilt-bronze is commonly used in China, and also called ormolu if it is Western. Methods of gilding include hand application and gluing, typically of gold leaf, chemical gilding, and electroplating, the last also called gold plating. Parcel-gilt objects are only gilded over part of their surfaces. This may mean that all of the inside, and none of the outside, of a chalice or similar vessel is gilded, or that patterns or images are made up by using a combination of gilt and ungilted areas."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Gilded_frame_being_burnished_with_agate_tool.jpg/330px-Gilded_frame_being_burnished_with_agate_tool.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gilding","wikidata":"Q1334300"},{"title":"Glamour","blocks":[{"text":"In the field of cultural studies, glamour is the impression of attraction or fascination that a particularly luxurious or elegant appearance creates, an impression which intensifies reality. Usually, a person, event, location, technology, or product such as a piece of clothing can be glamorous or add glamour. \"Glamour\" originally referred to a magic spell, an illusion said to be cast by witches."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Lana_Turner_by_Paul_Hesse_1946.jpg/330px-Lana_Turner_by_Paul_Hesse_1946.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glamour","wikidata":"Q390104"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Belt_Buckle_with_Zoomorphic_Design%2C_North_China%2C_3rd-2nd_century_BCE.jpg/500px-Belt_Buckle_with_Zoomorphic_Design%2C_North_China%2C_3rd-2nd_century_BCE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Belt_Buckle_with_Zoomorphic_Design%2C_North_China%2C_3rd-2nd_century_BCE.jpg/500px-Belt_Buckle_with_Zoomorphic_Design%2C_North_China%2C_3rd-2nd_century_BCE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Belt Buckle with nomadic-inspired zoomorphic design, manufactured in China for the Xiongnu. Mercury-gilded bronze (a Chinese technique invented by the Daoists in the 4th century CE). North China, 3rd–2nd century BC.[4][5]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABelt_Buckle_with_Zoomorphic_Design%2C_North_China%2C_3rd-2nd_century_BCE.jpg"},{"srcset"